3. Module Design Decisions and InterpretationsModule 1 Design DecisionsFixed Conversion Rate: Converted price_gbp to price_inr using the baseline constant $1\text{ GBP} = 105.50\text{ INR}$.Data Imputation & Parsing: Handled missing numeric fields via column median imputation. Text star ratings were mapped to integers ($1\text{ to }5$). Availability text was converted to integer booleans ($1\text{ for In Stock}, 0\text{ otherwise}$).Relational Schema: Implemented two normalized tables linked by primary and foreign keys: categories(category_id PK, category_name) and books(book_id PK, title, price_gbp, price_inr, rating, in_stock, category_id FK).Join Equivalence: Executed a multi-table join filtering for books with rating $\ge 4$ ordered by price. Confirmed that pd.read_sql and in-memory pd.merge produced identical DataFrames.Module 2 Analytical Findings & InterpretationsMissing Value Strategiesembarked / embark_town ($0.22\%$ missing): Dropped affected rows because missingness is well under the $5\%$ threshold.age ($19.87\%$ missing): Imputed using group medians computed across passenger class (pclass) and gender (sex), complying with the $5\%\text{--}30\%$ imputation window.deck ($77.22\%$ missing): Dropped column due to high sparsity ($>30\%$).Univariate Distribution & OutliersAge Outliers: $7$ outliers identified via IQR boundaries $[Q1 - 1.5 \times IQR, Q3 + 1.5 \times IQR]$.Fare Outliers: $116$ high-value outliers identified via IQR boundaries.Fare Skewness: Mean ($\approx 32.10$) $>$ Median ($14.45$) $>$ Mode ($8.05$). This ordering confirms a strong right-skewed distribution caused by premium first-class ticket purchases.Bivariate & Correlation AnalysisSurvival Rates:By Gender: Female = $74.20\%$, Male = $18.89\%$By Class: Class 1 = $62.96\%$, Class 2 = $47.28\%$, Class 3 = $24.24\%$Interactive Group: Class 1 Females = $96.81\%$, Class 3 Males = $13.54\%$Top 2 Off-Diagonal Absolute Correlations (6x6 Matrix):pclass vs. fare ($r = -0.549$): Strong inverse relationship reflecting that lower-numbered classes paid significantly higher fares.survived vs. pclass ($r = -0.338$): Negative correlation confirming that higher-tier passengers achieved substantially higher survival rates.(Redundant derived columns adult_male and alone were excluded).Multivariate Visual StoryChart 1 (Sex + Pclass Survival): Demonstrates that the "women and children first" evacuation protocol privileged females across all classes, though third-class females faced worse outcomes than first-class females.Chart 2 (Fare by Pclass and Survival): Shows survivors consistently paid higher fares within each socioeconomic class tier.Chart 3 (Age vs. Fare Scatter): Highlights that infant and young child survival was prioritized in classes 1 and 2, whereas working-age third-class males experienced the highest mortality rate.Chart 4 (Family Size vs. Survival): Demonstrates an inverted-U relationship where solo travelers and large families ($\ge 5$) had lower survival rates, while moderate family sizes ($2\text{--}4$) maximized survival.Exploratory Standardization CheckStandalone check confirmed transformed distributions had Mean $\approx 0.000$ and Standard Deviation $\approx 1.000$.Predictive Modeling & Imbalance ComparisonStratified Split: Stratified on survived ($38.38\%$ positive class) to prevent class distribution shifts between training and test sets.Data Leakage Prevention: All ColumnTransformer imputers, encoders, and scalers were fit on the training split only and applied via transform-only mode to the test split.Imbalance Comparison:Baseline: Precision = $0.793$, Recall = $0.697$, F1 = $0.742$class_weight='balanced': Precision = $0.737$, Recall = $0.788$, F1 = $0.762$SMOTE (Train Only): Precision = $0.725$, Recall = $0.758$, F1 = $0.741$Conclusion: class_weight='balanced' delivered the highest F1 score by elevating minority recall without requiring synthetic sample generation.Model Performance Comparison TableModelAccuracyPrecisionRecallF1-ScoreROC-AUCMAERMSER2Adjusted R2Logistic Regression0.8100.7930.6970.7420.854————Decision Tree0.7990.8200.6210.7070.821————Random Forest (Tuned)0.8320.8360.7270.7780.879————Multivariate Linear Regression—————19.3433.560.4120.392Hyperparameter Tuning: Tuned Random Forest (n_estimators=100, max_depth=6, max_features='sqrt') achieved an Out-Of-Bag (OOB) score of $0.8315$.Regression Heteroscedasticity: The residual plot exhibits an expanding funnel pattern, confirming heteroscedasticity due to high variance in luxury ticket fares.Deployment Recommendation: Deploy the Tuned Random Forest Pipeline. It leads across all classification metrics (Accuracy: $0.832$, F1: $0.778$, ROC-AUC: $0.879$), provides robustness against non-linear interactions, and generalizes reliably on raw passenger data.
